llest column\n   (\\(j=1\\)) contains exactly \\(m\\) points.\n\n   Construct an \\(m\\times m\\) array whose rows are the required sunny lines\n   and whose columns are the columns \\(C_{j}\\).  \n   In the entry \\((i,j)\\) (row \\(i\\), column \\(j\\)) we place the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{i,j}= \\bigl(j,\\; (i+j-1)\\bmod m +1\\bigr) .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties of the construction.*\n\n   * **(i) One point per column.**  \n     For a fixed column \\(j\\) the second coordinate in (1) runs through\n     the numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,m\\) as \\(i\\) varies, hence each column\n     receives exactly one point from each row.\n\n   * **(ii) No two points of the same row share a row, a column or a\n     diagonal.**  \n     Let two points of the same row \\(i\\) be \\(P_{i,j}\\) and\n     \\(P_{i,j'}\\) with \\(j\\neq j'\\).  \n     Their coordinates are  \n\n     \\[\n     P_{i,j}= \\bigl(j,\\; r_{j}\\bigr),\\qquad\n     P_{i,j'}= \\bigl(j',\\; r_{j'}\\bigr),\n     \\]\n     where \\(r_{j}= (i+j-1)\\bmod m +1\\).  \n     Because \\(j\\neq j'\\) we have \\(j\\neq j'\\) (different columns) and,\n     by the definition of \\(r_{j}\\), also \\(r_{j}\\neq r_{j'}\\) (different\n     rows).  \n     Moreover  \n\n     \\[\n     j+r_{j}\\equiv i-1\\pmod m,\\qquad\n     j'+r_{j'}\\equiv i-1\\pmod m,\n     \\]\n\n     so \\(j+r_{j}=j'+r_{j'}\\) would imply \\(j=j'\\), a contradiction.\n     Hence the sums of the coordinates are distinct, i.e. the two points\n     are not on a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n   Consequently each row of the array is a set of points in which no two\n   points lie on a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the\n   line \\(x+y=0\\).  Therefore the line determined by any two points of the\n   same row is *sunny*.  Because the points of a row are pairwise\n   non‑collinear (they have distinct columns), the whole row lies on a\n   single sunny line.  Distinct rows give distinct lines, because two\n   different rows contain point