            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must determine for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering every point of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n(a line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the\nline \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma  \n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct\nsunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le m+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_s^{(m)}:\\; y=-s\\,x+(m+s+1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_s^{(m)}\\) equals \\(-s\\); since \\(-s\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\), each\n\\(L_s^{(m)}\\) is sunny, and the \\(m\\) lines are pairwise distinct.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, then \\(b\\le m\\).  Choose \\(s=m-b+2\\) (which satisfies\n\\(2\\le s\\le m+1\\)).  Substituting \\(x=1\\) into (1) gives\n\\(y=m+s+1-s=m+1\\); hence \\((1,b)\\) lies on \\(L_s^{(m)}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 2\\)*, set  \n\n\\[\ns:=\\frac{m-b}{a-1}+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) we have \\(0\\le m-b\\le a-1\\); consequently\n\\(2\\le s\\le m+1\\) and \\(s\\) is an integer.  Substituting this value of \\(s\\) into\n(1) yields  \n\n\\[\ny=-\\Bigl(\\frac{m-b}{a-1}+1\\Bigr)a+(m+\\frac{m-b}{a-1}+2)=b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_s^{(m)}\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_m\\) lies on one\nof the \\(m\\) lines \\(\\{L_s^{(m)}\\mid 2\\le s\\le m+1\\}\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore non‑sunny.  They cover all\n   points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   The points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by (2) form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   R=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) (\\(1\\le a'\\le k\\)).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes\n   \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\); thus \\(R\\) is the translate (by \\(n-k\\) units to the right) of\n   the triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\n   Apply the Lemma with \\(m=k\\) to \\(T_k\\).  Translating each line \\(L_s^{(k)}\\) of\n   (1) by \\(n-k\\) units to the right gives the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s^{(k,n)}:\\; y=-s\\,x+\\bigl(k+s+1+s\\,(n-k)\\bigr),\\qquad 2\\le s\\le k+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Each line in (3) still has slope \\(-s\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); hence it is sunny, and\n   the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in R\\) we have \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) and, by the Lemma,\n   \\((a',b)\\) lies on \\(L_s^{(k)}\\) for some \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\).  Substituting\n   \\(a=a'+(n-k)\\) into the equation of \\(L_s^{(k,n)}\\) shows that \\((a,b)\\) also lies\n   on that same line.  Hence the lines (3) cover all points of \\(R\\).\n\n3. **Verification of the whole family.**  \n   The collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_s^{(k,n)}\\mid 2\\le s\\le k+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and\n   contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(L_s^{(k,n)}\\)).  \n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all\nthese values are attainable.  Clearly we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines\nbecause there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.  Therefore the set of admissible\n\\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\nAll non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) can occur:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
