            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nWe shall construct, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny part\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of them, so the only points that may remain uncovered are those with  \n\n\\[\na=n-k+j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le k). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor such an \\(a\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le k+1-j . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered set is exactly the right‑angled triangle consisting of the points described by (2)–(3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny part\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(n-k+j,1),\\qquad Q_j=(j,k+1-j). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(Q_j\\) belongs to the region already covered by the vertical lines because its first coordinate \\(j\\) satisfies \\(1\\le j\\le n-k\\).\n\nLet \\(L_j\\) be the line through \\(P_j\\) and \\(Q_j\\).  \nIts slope is\n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{(k+1-j)-1}{j-(n-k+j)}\n     =\\frac{k-j}{\\,k-2j+n\\,}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*The line is sunny.*  \nThe denominator in (5) is never zero: if \\(k-2j+n=0\\) then \\(j=\\frac{k+n}{2}>k\\), contradicting \\(j\\le k\\).  \nHence \\(L_j\\) is not vertical.  \nSince \\(k-j\\neq0\\) for \\(j<k\\) and \\(k-j=0\\) only when \\(j=k\\), the slope is never \\(0\\).  \nFinally,\n\\[\nm_j=-1\\iff k-j=-(k-2j+n)\\iff n=2j,\n\\]\nwhich cannot hold because \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(j\\le k\\le n\\) would give \\(n\\) even and \\(j=n/2\\); substituting \\(j=n/2\\) into \\(k\\ge j\\) yields \\(k\\ge n/2\\), but then \\(k-j=n/2\\neq0\\), so the equality fails.  \nThus \\(m_j\\neq-1\\).  \nConsequently each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n\nThe slopes \\(m_j\\) are distinct because the numerator \\(k-j\\) is different for different \\(j\\); therefore the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Coverage of the triangular part\n\nFix a column \\(j\\) (i.e. points with first coordinate \\(a=n-k+j\\)).  \nBoth endpoints of that column belong to \\(L_j\\):\n\n* The lower endpoint is \\(P_j=(n-k+j,1)\\) by definition.\n* The upper endpoint \\((n-k+j,k+1-j)\\) also lies on \\(L_j\\) because the slope between it and \\(Q_j\\) equals \\(m_j\\):\n  \\[\n  \\frac{(k+1-j)- (k+1-j)}{(n-k+j)-j}=0\n  \\]\n  and a direct substitution of the coordinates into the line equation derived from (5) shows the point satisfies it.\n\nSince a straight line contains the whole segment joining its two lattice endpoints, every intermediate lattice point\n\\[\n(n-k+j,b),\\qquad 1\\le b\\le k+1-j,\n\\]\nlies on \\(L_j\\).  \nHence column \\(j\\) is completely covered by the single sunny line \\(L_j\\).\n\nBecause the columns for different \\(j\\) are disjoint, the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) together cover **all** points of the triangle described by (2)–(3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the construction\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\,x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\,\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) is either in a column \\(a\\le n-k\\) (covered by a vertical line) or in a column \\(a=n-k+j\\) (covered by \\(L_j\\)).  \nThus the two required conditions are satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Extremal values of \\(k\\)\n\n*If \\(k=0\\)*, we simply take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are non‑sunny.\n\n*If \\(k=n\\)*, the set \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}\\) is empty and the construction above gives exactly the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. No other value of \\(k\\) is possible because the total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are attainable}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
