            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\).  \n\n**Vertical lines.**  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\,\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are distinct, non‑sunny, and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n**Uncovered region.**  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not covered by \\(\\mathcal V\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_k=\\Bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(n-k+j,\\;b\\bigr)\\;\\Big|\\;\nj=1,\\dots ,k,\\;b=1,\\dots ,k+1-j\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(\\Delta_k\\) is a right‑angled lattice triangle of side length \\(k\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y-1 = 1\\bigl(x-(n-k+j)\\bigr)\n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\ny = x-(n-k+j-1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_j\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore \\(L_j\\) is sunny (it is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nBecause the constants \\(-(n-k+j-1)\\) are distinct for different \\(j\\), the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\n*Points with \\(a\\le n-k\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le n-k\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n\n*Points of \\(\\Delta_k\\).*  \nFix \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-j\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nx=n-k+j+t,\\qquad\ny=1+t .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these coordinates into (3) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = (n-k+j+t)-(n-k+j-1)=1+t,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((n-k+j+t,\\;1+t)\\) belongs to \\(L_j\\).  \nWhen \\(t\\) runs from \\(0\\) to \\(k-j\\) we obtain exactly the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\, (n-k+j,\\;1),\\;(n-k+j+1,\\;2),\\dots ,(n,\\;k+1-j)\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is the whole column of \\(\\Delta_k\\) with first coordinate \\(n-k+j\\).  \nThus each column of the triangle is completely contained in the corresponding line \\(L_j\\); the columns are disjoint, so the union \\(\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}L_j\\) equals \\(\\Delta_k\\).\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal V\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting sunny lines\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal V\\) contains \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, while the set \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence the total number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is precisely the prescribed integer \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Extremal cases\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists solely of the \\(n\\) vertical lines (1); all are non‑sunny.\n* \\(k=n\\):  \\(\\mathcal V\\) is empty, and the \\(n