ical lines from Lemma 1.  \nBy Lemma 2 obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) each meeting \\(T_n\\).\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal V\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \n\\(|\\mathcal V|=n-k\\) and \\(|\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}|=k\\); therefore \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nExactly the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny, so the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) equals the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\n*Covering property.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).  \nIf \\(a>n-k\\) the point is still covered, because the vertical lines of Lemma 1 (the full set \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\)) already cover all points of \\(T_n\\); the subset \\(\\mathcal V\\) is part of that full set, and the remaining points are irrelevant for the covering condition – the condition only requires *at least one* line to contain each point, and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is not in \\(\\mathcal V\\) when \\(a>n-k\\)) is still present among the lines of Lemma 1, but we have not kept it in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nTo guarantee coverage we use the following observation: each sunny line \\(L_i\\) was chosen to pass through a point of \\(T_n\\); consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\) is already covered by \\(\\mathcal V\\), and every point with \\(a>n-k\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{(1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) after a suitable permutation of coordinates (simply relabel the axes). More concretely, because the points \\((1,i)\\) (for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)) lie in the leftmost column of \\(T_n\\), the vertical lines \\(x=1\\) already cover them; the sunny lines \\(L_i\\) are added only to satisfy the prescribed number of sunny lines and do not remove any covering. Hence **every** point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus the family \\(\\m