            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny covering\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have\n\\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\) of one of the lines (1).  \nThus the family (1) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exist \\(n\\) **sunny** lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Because a sunny line is not vertical*, each line meets the first column\n\\(x=1\\) in a unique point; denote this point by \\((1,i)\\) for some\n\\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Distinct lines give distinct points, hence after a\nrenumbering we may assume that \\(\\ell_i\\) contains \\((1,i)\\).\n\n*Because a sunny line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)*, it cannot contain two\ndifferent points of the hypotenuse \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Consequently each line meets\nthe hypotenuse in exactly one point; write this point as\n\\((h_i,\\,n+1-h_i)\\) with \\(h_i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Again the\n\\(h_i\\)’s are all distinct, so \\((h_i)_{i=1}^n\\) is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) is determined by the two points\n\\((1,i)\\) and \\((\\sigma(i),\\,n+1-\\sigma(i))\\); its slope equals  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{\\,n+1-\\sigma(i)-i\\,}{\\sigma(i)-1}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(i=n\\) then the numerator of (2) is \\(1-\\sigma(n)\\) and the denominator\nis \\(\\sigma(n)-1\\); therefore \\(m_n=-1\\) **for every possible value of\n\\(\\sigma(n)\\)**.  This contradicts the hypothesis that \\(\\ell_n\\) is sunny.\nHence a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n#### 3.1  The non‑sunny part  \n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\,\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose\nfirst coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n#### 3.2  The remaining triangular region  \n\nThe points that are still uncovered are those with first coordinate\n\\(x\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); they form the set  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we translate the picture by the vector \\((-(n-k),0)\\) we obtain exactly the\nset  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(u,v)\\mid u\\ge 1,\\;v\\ge 1,\\;u+v\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe triangle that corresponds to the integer \\(k\\).  Hence \\(|T_k'|=|T_k|\n=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n#### 3.3  \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k'\\)\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = j\\,x - j\\,(n-k). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*   The slope of \\(L_j\\) is \\(j\\); because \\(j\\ge 1\\) we have\n    \\(j\\neq 0\\) and \\(j\\neq -1\\).  The line is not vertical, so it is sunny.\n*   Write a point of the column \\(x=n-k+u\\) (\\(u\\ge 1\\)).  Substituting\n    \\(x=n-k+u\\) into (4) gives  \n\n    \\[\n    y = j\\,(n-k+u)-j\\,(n-k)=j\\,u . \\tag{5}\n    \\]\n\n    Thus the intersection of \\(L_j\\) with the column \\(x=n-k+u\\) is the point\n    \\((n-k+u,\\;j\\,u)\\).\n\n    In column \\(x=n-k+u\\) the admissible \\(y\\)-coordinates are\n    \\(1,2,\\dots ,k+1-u\\) (because the condition\n    \\((n-k+u)+y\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to \\(y\\le k+1-u\\)).  \n    When \\(j\\) runs through \\(1,\\dots ,k\\) the numbers \\(j\\,u\\) run exactly\n    through this set: indeed \\(j\\,u\\le k\\,u\\) and, because \\(u\\le k\\),\n    the values \\(j\\,u\\) with \\(j=1,\\dots ,k+1-u\\) give all integers\n    \\(1,\\dots ,k+1-u\\), while the remaining values exceed the bound and are\n    irrelevant.\n\nConsequently every point of \\(T_k'\\) lies on precisely one of the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n#### 3.4  The final family  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal V\\;\\cup\\;\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and\n\\(k\\) sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nBy the discussion in Sections 3.1 and 3.3, every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to\nat least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Moreover exactly the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced a configuration satisfying both\nrequirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) the construction (6) yields\n  \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* By Lemma 2 a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines is impossible for any\n  \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n