            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (All‑sunny covering)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 0\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose\nunion contains \\(S_{m}\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n*Base.*  \n\\(m=0\\): \\(S_{0}=\\varnothing\\) – the empty family works.  \n\\(m=1\\): \\(S_{1}=\\{(1,1)\\}\\); the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and\ncontains the point.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for \\(m-1\\) (\\(m\\ge 2\\)).\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{m}:\\; y = x-(m-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{m}\\) is sunny because its slope is \\(1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m\\),\n\n\\[\n(t,m-t)\\in S_{m}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (t,m-t)\\in L_{m},\n\\]\n\nso \\(L_{m}\\) contains exactly the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{m}:=\\{(t,m-t)\\mid 1\\le t\\le m\\}\\subset S_{m}.\n\\]\n\nRemoving this diagonal from \\(S_{m}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nS_{m}\\setminus D_{m}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m\\}=S_{m-1}.\n\\]\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there are \\(m-1\\) distinct sunny lines covering\n\\(S_{m-1}\\). None of those lines coincides with \\(L_{m}\\) because all have\ndifferent intercepts (the intercepts of the covering of \\(S_{m-1}\\) belong\nto \\(\\{-(m-2),\\dots ,0\\}\\), whereas \\(L_{m}\\) has intercept \\(-(m-1)\\)).\nHence the family consisting of those \\(m-1\\) lines together with \\(L_{m}\\)\ncontains \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_{m}\\).\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Reduction)\n\nLet \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and let \\(v,h\\) be non‑negative integers with\n\\(v+h=n-k\\).\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{n}\\mid a>v,\\;b>h\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen the map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-v,b-h)\\) is a bijection from \\(T\\) onto\n\\(S_{k}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\((a,b)\\in T\\) set \\(a'=a-v,\\;b'=b-h\\).  \nSince \\(a>v,\\;b>h\\) we have \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\), and\n\n\\[\na'+b'=(a+b)-(v+h)\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a',b')\\in S_{k}\\).  \nConversely, given \\((a',b')\\in S_{k}\\) put \\(a=a'+v,\\;b=b'+h\\); then\n\\(a\\le v+(k+1-b')\\le v+h+k=n\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\in T\\).\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nChoose \\(v=n-k,\\;h=0\\) (any pair with \\(v+h=n-k\\) works).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nTake the \\(v\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=v .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and they\ncover every point of \\(S_{n}\\) whose \\(a\\)-coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le v\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma 2, the points of \\(S_{n}\\) not covered by (4) are precisely the\nset \\(S_{k}\\) after the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-v,b)\\).\nLemma 1 applied with \\(m=k\\) yields \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering\n\\(S_{k}\\); translating them back gives \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering\nthe uncovered points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nAll together we have \\(v+h+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at\nleast one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nSince there are only \\(n\\) lines in total, the number of sunny lines cannot\nexceed \\(n\\).  Thus \\(k>n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration satisfying the\ntwo required conditions exists, and no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
