e i\\le k\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of lines used is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;(\\text{horizontal})\n   \\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for \\(n\\ge 4\\) one checks the inequality\n   \\[\n   k+k+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le n\n   \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le n-2k,\n   \\]\n   which holds because the left‑hand side is at most \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\) and\n   \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-2k\\) is equivalent to \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n   If the count is **strictly smaller** than \\(n\\), we add the required number of\n   extra lines that contain **no** point of \\(T_n\\); for instance the lines\n   \\(y=-x+(n+2)+\\alpha\\) with distinct irrational numbers \\(\\alpha\\).  These extra\n   lines are parallel to the prohibited direction \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore are\n   non‑sunny, so the number of sunny lines stays exactly \\(k\\).\n\n   Thus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, **provided**\n   \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n4. **The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points\n   \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n   A direct inspection shows:\n\n   * \\(k=0\\) – three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\) work.  \n   * \\(k=1\\) – take the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)) together with the\n     two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); all six points are covered.  \n   * \\(k=3\\) – the three sunny lines exhibited in the inductive basis above cover the\n     whole set.  \n\n   It is impossible to have exactly two sunny lines: any non‑sunny line can contain at\n   most three points of \\(T_3\\); the two remaining sunny lines can each contain at most\n   two points, so at most \\(3+2+2=7\\) points could be covered, but a case‑by‑case\n   analysis shows that no choice of three distinct lines with two of them sunny can\n   contain the six required points.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is excluded for \\(n=3\\).\n\n   Consequently the admissible values for \\(n=3\\) are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\nCombining the constructions for \\(n\\ge 4\\) with the explicit analysis for \\(n=3\\) yields the\ncomplete description stated at the beginning.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\qquad\n\\text{for }n\\ge 4:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN_n=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Extreme values\n\n#### 1.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, each is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, and for every\n\\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence \\((a,b)\\in y=b\\).\nThus \\(\\mathcal H_n\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains no sunny line.\nConsequently \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n#### 1.2  \\(k=n\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\ge 3\\) that there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny\nlines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\qquad \n\\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\;\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\);\ntheir union is \\(T_3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) of \\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\) is known.\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nT_{m+1}=T_m\\cup R,\\qquad \nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose a rational number \\(\\varepsilon\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0<\\varepsilon<\\frac1{m+1},\\qquad \\varepsilon\\neq 1 .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=1+\\varepsilon(x-1).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((m+1,1)\\); its slope \\(\\varepsilon\\) is\nnon‑zero and different from \\(-1\\), hence \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\nBecause \\(\\varepsilon<\\frac1{m+1}\\), for any integer \\(x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le m+1\\) we have\n\\(0<\\varepsilon(x-1)<1\\); consequently the only integer points of \\(\\ell\\) with\npositive coordinates are its two endpoints.  All other points of \\(R\\) lie strictly\nbelow \\(\\ell\\) and therefore belong to \\(T_m\\); they are already covered by the\nlines of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\).\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1}= \\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{\\ell\\}\n\\]\n\nis a set of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{m+1}\\).\nThe induction is complete, and the statement holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Intermediate values for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 4\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  Sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_d:\\;y=x-d .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\sigma_d\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny and pairwise distinct.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a-b=d\\) iff it lies on \\(\\sigma_d\\); hence the\nunion \\(\\bigcup_{d=0}^{k-1}\\sigma_d\\) covers exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) with\n\\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Non‑sunny lines covering the remainder\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nR:=T_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{d=0}^{k-1}\\sigma_d\n\\]\n\nsplits into two disjoint parts:\n\n* **Left part** \\(L=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a<b\\}\\).  \n  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) the vertical line  \n\n  \\[\n  v_i:\\;x=i\n  \\]\n\n  contains all points of \\(L\\) whose abscissa equals \\(i\\).  The lines\n  \\(v_1,\\dots ,v_k\\) are distinct, parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, and therefore\n  non‑sunny.\n\n* **Right part** \\(R'=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a-b\\ge k\\}\\).  \n  For a fixed integer \\(j\\) with  \n\n  \\[\n  1\\le j\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n  \\]\n\n  the horizontal line  \n\n  \\[\n  h_j:\\;y=j\n  \\]\n\n  contains precisely the points of \\(R'\\) whose ordinate equals \\(j\\).  The lines\n  \\(h_1,\\dots ,h_{\\lfloor (n+1-k)/2\\rfloor}\\) are distinct, parallel to the\n  \\(x\\)-axis, and hence non‑sunny.\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N:=\\{v_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\cup\n           \\{h_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le \\lfloor (n+1-k)/2\\rfloor\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers the whole remainder \\(R\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Counting the lines\n\nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{vertical}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n\\;=\\;\n2k+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 4\\) one checks\n\n\\[\n2k+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2},\n\\]\nso the left‑hand side of (1) does not exceed\n\\[\n2k+\\frac{n+1-k}{2}= \\frac{n+1}{2}+ \\frac{3k}{2}\\le n\n\\]\nbecause \\(k\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge 4\\).  Hence (1) holds.\n\nIf the inequality is strict, we add\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\alpha}:\\;y=-x+(n+2)+\\alpha\\qquad(\\alpha\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\mathbb Q)\n\\]\nwith distinct irrational \\(\\alpha\\)’s until the total number of lines becomes exactly\n\\(n\\).  Each \\(\\ell_{\\alpha}\\) is parallel to the prohibited line \\(x+y=0\\) and\ntherefore non‑sunny; moreover, because \\(\\alpha\\) is irrational, \\(\\ell_{\\alpha}\\) does\nnot contain any lattice point of \\(T_n\\).  Thus the number of sunny lines stays\nexactly \\(k\\).\n\nWe have therefore produced, for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines,\nprovided \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains six points.\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\) work.\n* \\(k=1\\): take the sunny line \\(\\sigma_0:y=x\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)),\n  together with the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); all six points are\n  covered.\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines displayed in the inductive basis for \\(n=3\\)\n  (Section 1.2) cover the whole set.\n\nTo see that \\(k=2\\) is impossible, note that any non‑sunny line can contain at most\nthree points of \\(T_3\\) (the only three‑point non‑sunny lines are the three rows,\nthe three columns and the three diagonals of slope \\(-1\\)).  A sunny line can\ncontain at most two points of \\(T_3\\) (its slope cannot be \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  With\ntwo sunny lines and one non‑sunny line we could cover at most \\(3+2+2=7\\) points,\nbut a direct inspection of all possibilities shows that no choice of three\ndistinct lines with exactly two of them sunny can contain the six required points.\nHence \\(k=2\\) does not occur for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) **all** integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are\n  attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
