y  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\},\\qquad \n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of lines used is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;(\\text{horizontal})\n   \\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for \\(n\\ge 4\\) one checks the inequality\n   \\[\n   k+k+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le n\n   \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le n-2k,\n   \\]\n   which holds because the left\u2011hand side is at most \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\) and\n   \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-2k\\) is equivalent to \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n   If the count is **strictly smaller** than \\(n\\), we add the required number of\n   extra lines that contain **no** point of \\(T_n\\); for instance the lines\n   \\(y=-x+(n+2)+\\alpha\\) with distinct irrational numbers \\(\\alpha\\).  These extra\n   lines are parallel to the prohibited direction \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore are\n   non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines stays exactly \\(k\\).\n\n   Thus for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines, **provided**\n   \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n4. **The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points\n   \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n   A direct inspection shows:\n\n   * \\(k=0\\) \u2013 three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\) work.  \n   * \\(k=1\\) \u2013 take the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)) together with the\n     two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); all six points are covered.  \n   * \\(k=3\\) \u2013 the three sunny lines exhibited in the inductive basis above cover the\n     whole set.  \n\n   It is impossible to have exactly two sunny lines: any non\u2011sunny line can