h \\(1\\le a\\le i\\); therefore \\(|L_i\\cap S_n|=i\\le M\\).\n\nNow replace the diagonal \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) by the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) and delete any \\(k-1\\) of the remaining diagonals\n\\(\\ell_c\\;(c\\neq n+1)\\).  Each deleted diagonal loses at most \\(M\\) points,\nbut for every deleted diagonal we have a sunny line \\(L_i\\) that already\ncovers at least one of its points (namely the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) belonging to\n\\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) and also to \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\)).  Consequently after the\nreplacement every point of \\(S_n\\) is still covered.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{all diagonals except }\\ell_{n+1}}\n\\;+\\;k\\;-\\;(k-1)=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Hence for odd \\(n\\) all values\n\\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n\\) are realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  The case \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) (even \\(n\\)).\n\nLet \\(n=2m\\) (so \\(M=m\\)).  \nProceed as in §3.2, but now we are allowed to delete at most \\(k-1\\) diagonals,\nbecause we must keep one non‑sunny line (otherwise we would have \\(k=n\\),\nwhich is impossible by §2).  Choose the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=1\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nas the remaining non‑sunny line; it contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\nApply the construction of §3.2 with the same set of sunny lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) and delete \\(k-1\\) diagonals different from \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\).\nAll points not on the vertical line are covered exactly as before, while the\nvertical line supplies the \\(n\\) points that would otherwise be missing if we\nhad tried to use \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.  The total number of lines is again \\(n\\),\nand precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be achieved,\nand \\(k=n\\) cannot.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the impossibility proved in §2 with the explicit constructions of\n§3 we obtain the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny\nlines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThis finishes the solution."
