S_n\\) in at most \\(\\displaystyle\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\nWrite a line in the form  \n\n\\[\nax+by=c ,\\qquad (a,b)\\neq(0,0).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe three forbidden directions correspond to \\((a,b)\\) being proportional to\n\\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,1)\\).  \nHence for a sunny line we have \\(a\\neq0\\), \\(b\\neq0\\) and \\(a+b\\neq0\\).\n\nIntroduce the change of variables  \n\n\\[\nu=x+y,\\qquad v=x-y .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIn the \\((u,v)\\)-plane the set \\(S_n\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\{(u,v)\\mid u\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\;|v|\\le u-2\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2) rewrites as  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{a+b}{2}\\,u+\\frac{a-b}{2}\\,v=c .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny we have \\(a+b\\neq0\\); consequently the coefficient of\n\\(u\\) in (5) is non\u2011zero, i.e. the line is **not** parallel to the \\(u\\)-axis.\nHence for each integer value of \\(u\\) there is at most one integer \\(v\\) that\nsatisfies (5).  The admissible integers \\(u\\) are \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); among\nthem at most every second one can occur on the line, because the parity of\n\\(u\\) is forced by (5).  Therefore the number of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on a\nsunny line does not exceed  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor = M .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even is impossible.\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  By (6) each of them contains at most\n\\(M\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  For even \\(n\\) we have \\(M=n/2\\); consequently the\ntotal number of covered points is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2>\\frac{n^{2}}2\\) for every even \\(n\\ge2\\).  Hence a\ncovering by \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The case \\(k=0\\).\n\nThe \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny, and for each integer \\(c\\) the equation \\(x+y=c\\) yields\nexactly the points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a+b=c\\).  Hence (7) covers the whole set\n\\(S_n\\) with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  General construction for any admissible \\(k\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(k\\neq n\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even}).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  The points of\n\\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\) are all covered by the family\n\\(\\{V_i\\}\\).\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 the uncovered part.*  \nThe points that remain uncovered form the right\u2011angled triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=x+(n-2i+1).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  Solving the\nsystem \\(\\{x+y=n-i+1,\\;y=x+(n-2i+1)\\}\\) shows that the points of \\(T_k\\) are\npartitioned as  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\bigl(L_i\\cap T_k\\bigr),\\qquad\nL_i\\cap T_k=\\{(n-k+i+j,\\;i+j)\\mid j=0,1,\\dots ,\\min\\{M-i,\\,k-i\\}\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus each \\(L_i\\) contains at most \\(M\\) points of \\(T_k\\) (by (6)), and every\npoint of \\(T_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 total family.*  \nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}= \\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\