m  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_k\\) is a right‑angled integer triangle of side length\n\\(k\\); a direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.3  Sunny lines of slope 1 covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;y = x + (1-n+j).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(L_j\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\nt:=n+1-(a+b)\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the integer \\(t\\) is well defined.  A short\ncomputation shows  \n\n\\[\nb-a = (1-n)+t .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nEquation (13) is precisely the equation of the line \\(L_t\\) given in (11);\nhence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_t\\).  Thus every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on\nexactly one of the lines \\(\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\\).\n\n#### 3.4  The whole family  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains \\( (n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines; exactly the\n\\(k\\) lines \\(L_j\\) are sunny.  By construction the vertical lines cover\nall points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\), while the sunny lines cover the\nremaining points \\(T_k\\).  Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at\nleast one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7) a configuration of \\(n\\) lines\nwith precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final classification\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd.*  \nThen \\(M=\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\) and the construction with \\(k=n\\) (i.e. no\nvertical lines) gives the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,L_j : y = x + (1-n+j),\\; j=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines and covers all of \\(S_n\\).  Together\nwi