            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(\\displaystyle\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points.\n\nWrite a sunny line in the form \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).\nIntroduce the linear change of variables  \n\n\\[\nu=a+b,\\qquad v=a-b .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\) are integers, \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) have the same parity.\nFrom (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(u,v)\\mid u\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\;|v|\\le u-2,\\;u\\equiv v\\pmod2\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(a=\\frac{u+v}{2},\\;b=\\frac{u-v}{2}\\) into \\(y=mx+b\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{u-v}{2}=m\\frac{u+v}{2}+b\n\\Longrightarrow (1-m)u+(-1-m)v=2b .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince the line is sunny we have \\(m\\neq1\\); therefore the coefficient\n\\(1-m\\) of \\(u\\) in (4) is non‑zero.  Consequently, for each integer\n\\(v\\) there is at most one integer \\(u\\) satisfying (4), and for each\ninteger \\(u\\) there is at most one integer \\(v\\) (the parity condition\n(3) forces \\(v\\equiv u\\pmod2\\)).  Hence a sunny line can contain at most\none point of \\(S_n\\) for each admissible value of \\(u\\).\n\nThe admissible values of \\(u\\) are the \\(n\\) integers\n\\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  Because of the parity restriction, at most every\nsecond one can occur on a given line; therefore the number of points of\n\\(S_n\\) lying on a sunny line does not exceed  \n\n\\[\nM=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\) is impossible for even \\(n\\).\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  By (5) each line contains at most\n\\(M\\) points of \\(S_n\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le nM=\n\\begin{cases}\nn\\cdot\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\dfrac{n(n+1)}2, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[4pt]\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\dfrac{n^{2}}2, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor even \\(n\\) we have \\(\\dfrac{n^{2}}2<\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|\\); thus the\ninequality cannot be an equality, and a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines is\nimpossible.  Consequently, when \\(n\\) is even the number \\(k=n\\) cannot be\nrealised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n,\\qquad\n(k\\neq n\\text{ if }n\\text{ is even}).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.1  Non‑sunny vertical lines  \n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  All points of\n\\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\) lie on one of the lines\n\\(V_i\\).\n\n#### 3.2  The uncovered triangle  \n\nThe points not yet covered form  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_k\\) is a right‑angled integer triangle of side length\n\\(k\\); a direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.3  Sunny lines of slope 1 covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;y = x + (1-n+j).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(L_j\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore each \\(L_j\\) is sunny.\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\nt:=n+1-(a+b)\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the integer \\(t\\) is well defined.  A short\ncomputation shows  \n\n\\[\nb-a = (1-n)+t .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nEquation (13) is precisely the equation of the line \\(L_t\\) given in (11);\nhence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_t\\).  Thus every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on\nexactly one of the lines \\(\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\\).\n\n#### 3.4  The whole family  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains \\( (n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines; exactly the\n\\(k\\) lines \\(L_j\\) are sunny.  By construction the vertical lines cover\nall points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\), while the sunny lines cover the\nremaining points \\(T_k\\).  Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at\nleast one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7) a configuration of \\(n\\) lines\nwith precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final classification\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd.*  \nThen \\(M=\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\) and the construction with \\(k=n\\) (i.e. no\nvertical lines) gives the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,L_j : y = x + (1-n+j),\\; j=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines and covers all of \\(S_n\\).  Together\nwith the constructions for smaller \\(k\\) we obtain every integer\n\\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even.*  \nStep 2 shows that \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.  The construction of Section 3\nworks for all \\(k\\le n-1\\).  Hence the admissible values are\n\\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
