  \\]\n\n   Hence for even \\(n\\) the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n4. **Explicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Choose* an integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n,\\qquad k\\neq n\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   - **(i) Non\u2011sunny vertical lines.**  \n     Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k). \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     They cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies\n     \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n   - **(ii) Remaining points.**  \n     The uncovered set is  \n\n     \\[\n     T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     Its cardinality is \\(|T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n   - **(iii) Sunny lines of slope\u202f1 covering \\(T_k\\).**  \n     For each \\(j\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n\n     \\[\n     L_j:\\;y = x + (1-n+j). \\tag{5}\n     \\]\n\n     Every \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is therefore sunny.  \n     Let \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Set  \n\n     \\[\n     t:=n+1-(a+b)\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}. \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n\n     Then \\(b-a = (1-n)+t\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_t\\).\n     Consequently each point of \\(T_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines\n     \\(\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\\).\n\n   - **(iv) The whole family.**  \n     \\[\n     \\mathcal F=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}.\n     \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n\n     \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely the \\(k\\) lines\n     \\(L_j\\) are sunny.  By (i)\u2013(iii) every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least\n     one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n5. **Completion of the classification.**  \n\n   - If \\(n\\) is odd, (1) gives \\(M=\\dfrac{n+1}{2}\\) and the construction with\n     \\(k=n\\) (i.e. no vertical lines) works, providing a family of \\(n\\) sunny\n     lines.  Together with the families for smaller \\(k\\) we obtain 