"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nFor an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Re\u2011encoding the required points.**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   Introduce the linear change of variables  \n   \\[\n   u=a+b,\\qquad v=a-b .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The inverse transformation is \\(a=\\frac{u+v}{2},\\;b=\\frac{u-v}{2}\\).  \n   From the definition of \\(S_n\\) we obtain the equivalent description  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &2\\le u\\le n+1,\\\\\n   &|v|\\le u-2,\\\\\n   &u\\equiv v\\pmod 2 .\n   \\end{aligned}}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **A universal upper bound for a sunny line.**  \n\n   *Sunny* means that the slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   Any non\u2011vertical line can be written \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \n   Substituting \\(a=\\frac{u+v}{2},\\;b=\\frac{u-v}{2}\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{u-v}{2}=m\\frac{u+v}{2}+c\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   (1-m)u+(-1-m)v=2c .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\((a,b)\\) are integers, both \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) are integers; consequently the left\u2011hand side of (3) is an integer. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   2c\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Write the slope as a reduced fraction \\(m=p/q\\) with \\(q>0\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \n   Multiplying (3) by \\(q\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   (q-p)u+(-q-p)v=2cq .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The coefficients \\((q-p)\\) and \\((-q-p)\\) are coprime (any common divisor would also divide their sum \\(2q\\) and difference \\(-2p\\), hence would divide \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)).  \n   Therefore for each integer value of \\(v\\) there is **at most one** integer \\(u\\) satisfying (5); if such a \\(u\\) exists it is uniquely determined by  \n\n   \\[\n   u=\\frac{2cq+ (q+p)v}{\\,q-p\\,}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The same conclusion holds when \\(m=1\\) (i.e. \\(p=q\\)): then (3) reduces to \\(v=-2c\\), fixing \\(v\\) and leaving \\(u\\) free.\n\n   Now we use the parity condition in (2).  \n   Because \\(u\\equiv v\\pmod 2\\), the admissible pairs \\((u,v)\\) have the same parity.  \n   For a fixed line, the relation (5) forces a **linear** relation between \\(u\\) and \\(v\\); consequently the parity of \\(u\\) is forced once the parity of \\(v\\) is chosen.  \n   Hence among the integers \\(u\\) in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) at most **one half** can appear on the line (the other half would have the opposite parity and thus violate (2)).  \n\n   The interval \\([2,n+1]\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) integers; therefore any sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\). This bound is valid for every possible slope \\(m\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\) and for any real intercept \\(c\\).\n\n3. **Consequences for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n   The non\u2011sunny lines (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)) can each contain arbitrarily many points, so the only restriction comes from the sunny lines.  \n   Using the bound (7),\n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\;=\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\le\\;kM+(n-k)\\cdot |S_n|\n   \\]\n\n   is trivially satisfied; the relevant inequality is the *maximum* number of points that can be supplied by the sunny lines alone:\n\n   \\[\n   kM\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|-\\text{(points covered by the }(n-k)\\text{ non\u2011sunny lines)} .\n   \\]\n\n   In the construction below we will make the non\u2011sunny lines vertical, each covering a whole column of points, so the only points that must be supplied by sunny lines are those in the right\u2011most triangular region of size \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).  \n   For the *extreme* case \\(k=n\\) we would need the sunny lines alone to cover all \\(|S_n|\\) points.  \n   Comparing \\(|S_n|\\) with the maximal possible contribution \\(nM\\) gives\n\n   \\[\n   nM=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n\\cdot\\frac{n+1}{2}=|S_n|, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[4pt]\n   n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\dfrac{n^{2}}2<\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|, & n\\text{ even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **if \\(n\\) is even, \\(k=n\\) is impossible**; for odd \\(n\\) the bound does not forbid \\(k=n\\).\n\n4. **Explicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n,\\qquad k\\neq n\\text{ when }n\\text{ is even}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   *4.1  Non\u2011sunny vertical lines.*  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on exactly one of the \\(V_i\\).\n\n   *4.2  The uncovered set.*  \n   The points not yet covered form  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   A direct count gives  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   *4.3  Sunny lines of slope\u202f1 covering \\(T_k\\).*  \n   For each integer  \n\n   \\[\n   j\\in\\Bigl\\{-(n-k),\\;-(n-k)+2,\\;-(n-k)+4,\\dots ,-(n-k)+2(k-1)\\Bigr\\}\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_j:\\;y = x + j .\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \n   The set in (13) contains exactly \\(k\\) distinct integers; denote them\n   \\(j_0,j_1,\\dots ,j_{k-1}\\).\n\n   **Claim.**  \n   \\[\n   T_k=\\bigcup_{t=0}^{k-1} L_{j_t}.\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) and put \\(d:=b-a\\).  \n   From the inequalities defining \\(T_k\\),\n\n   \\[\n   a\\ge n-k+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   -(n-k)\\le d\\le -(n-k)+2(k-1).\n   \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, because \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers, \\(d\\) has the same parity as\n   \\(-(n-k)\\): indeed,\n   \\[\n   d=b-a\\equiv (a+b)-(2a)\\equiv (a+b)\\pmod 2,\n   \\]\n   and from \\(a+b\\equiv a-b\\pmod 2\\) we obtain \\(d\\equiv -(n-k)\\pmod 2\\).  \n   Conditions (16) together with this parity requirement mean precisely that\n   \\(d\\) belongs to the arithmetic progression (13); hence \\(d=j_t\\) for a unique\n   index \\(t\\).  Since \\(b-a=j_t\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of\n   \\(L_{j_t}\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_{j_t}\\).\n\n   Conversely, suppose \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b-a=j_t\\) for some \\(t\\).  \n   Then \\(a+b = 2a + j_t\\).  \n   Because \\(j_t\\) has the same parity as \\(-(n-k)\\) and \\(2a\\) is even, the sum\n   \\(a+b\\) has the same parity as \\(n+1\\); together with the bounds\n   \\(- (n-k)\\le j_t\\le -(n-k)+2(k-1)\\) one checks that\n   \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) hold, so \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  \n   Hence (15) is proved. \u220e\n\n   *4.4  The whole family.*  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_{j_0},\\dots ,L_{j_{k-1}}\\}.\n   \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely the\n   \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{j_t}\\) are sunny.  \n   By construction, every point of \\(S_n\\) lies either on a vertical line\n   \\(V_i\\) (if its first coordinate is \\(\\le n-k\\)) or on one of the lines\n   \\(L_{j_t}\\) (if its first coordinate is \\(\\ge n-k+1\\)).  Thus the covering\n   requirement is satisfied.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   * If \\(n\\) is odd, the bound (8) shows that \\(k=n\\) is not ruled out, and the\n     construction above works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n   * If \\(n\\) is even, (8) proves that \\(k=n\\) cannot be achieved; the construction\n     works for all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one stated\n   in the boxed answer. \u220e"
