 \\[\n   a\\ge n-k+1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   -(n-k)\\le d\\le -(n-k)+2(k-1).\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) have the same parity, hence \\(d\\equiv -(n-k)\\pmod 2\\).  \n   Conditions (15) together with this parity requirement mean precisely that\n   \\(d\\) belongs to the arithmetic progression (12); therefore \\(d=j_t\\) for a\n   unique index \\(t\\).  Since \\(b-a=j_t\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the\n   equation of \\(L_{j_t}\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_{j_t}\\).\n\n   Conversely, if \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b-a=j_t\\) for some \\(t\\), then\n   \\(a+b = 2a + j_t\\).  Because \\(j_t\\) has the same parity as \\(-(n-k)\\) and\n   \\(2a\\) is even, we obtain \\(a+b\\equiv n+1\\pmod 2\\).  Together with the\n   bounds \\(-(n-k)\\le j_t\\le -(n-k)+2(k-1)\\) one checks that\n   \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  \u220e\n\n   *4.4  The whole family.*  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_{j_0},\\dots ,L_{j_{k-1}}\\}.\n   \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely\n   the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{j_t}\\) are sunny.  \n   By (9) every point with first coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\) lies on a vertical line,\n   and by the claim (14) every remaining point lies on one of the lines\n   \\(L_{j_t}\\).  Hence **all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered**.\n\n5. **The case \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).  \n   Take the \\(n\\) lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,L_i:\\;y = -\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{i}{i+1}\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,2m)\\,}.\n   \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) the line passes through the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(1,i+1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+2,1),\n   \\]\n\n   and, because the step in the \\(u\\)-coordinate equals \\(2(i+1)\\), it contains\n   exactly \\(\\dfrac{n+1}{2}=m+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The sets \\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\mid i=0,\\dots ,2m\\}\\) are precis