 (3).  \n   The remaining triangle has size \\(k\\) (which is even because \\(k\\le n-1\\)).  \n   By Lemma 2 it can be covered with \\(k-1\\) sunny lines; together with the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines we obtain a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Since \\(k\\) may be any integer from \\(0\\) to \\(n-1\\), all those values occur.\n\n   *Odd \\(n\\).*  \n   If \\(k\\le n-2\\) we proceed as for the even case, taking \\(t=n-k\\) vertical lines; the remaining triangle has size \\(k\\) (odd), and Lemma 1 gives a covering with \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   For \\(k=n\\) we simply apply Lemma 1 to the whole triangle \\(T_n\\).  \n   The case \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible: after removing any single non‑sunny line we are left with a triangle of even size \\(n-1\\), which by Lemma 2 cannot be covered with all its lines sunny. Hence \\(k=n-1\\) does not occur.\n\n   Consequently the set of attainable \\(k\\) is exactly the one described in (2).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nFor a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) write it as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\neq\\infty\\).  \nThe integer points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1,\\qquad b=ma+c .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(m\\) is rational, say \\(m=p/q\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), then the lattice points on \\(\\ell\\) form an arithmetic progression with step \\((q,p)\\).  \nInside the triangle the progression can contain at most  \n\n\\[\nM_n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints; indeed the longest such progression is obtained for slope \\(m=1\\) (or \\(m=-1\\) which is forbidden), giving the bound (1).\n\nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or of the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \nEach of these contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsequently, if a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, then\n\n\\[\nkM_n+(n-k)n\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(M_n-n\\bigr)+\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\ge0 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(M_n-n\\le0\\), (2) yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn      &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn-1    &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k=n\\) is impossible for even \\(n\\); all other values are not excluded by (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Two covering lemmas\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }m\\ge3\\text{ is odd, }T_m\\text{ can be covered by }m\\text{ sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Induction on the odd integer \\(m\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=3\\).*  The three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+2\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively the point‑sets \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\), \\(\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\); together they cover all six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  Let \\(m\\ge5\\) be odd.  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=-2x+m+1\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npasses through the three lattice points \\((1,m),(2,m-2),(3,m-4)\\), all belonging to \\(T_m\\); its slope \\(-2\\) is sunny.  \nRemoving these three points leaves the triangle \\(T_{m-2}\\).  \nBy the induction hypothesis \\(T_{m-2}\\) can be covered with \\(m-2\\) sunny lines.  \nAdding \\(\\ell\\) gives a covering of \\(T_m\\) with \\(m\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{If }m\\ge4\\text{ is even, }T_m\\text{ can be covered by }m-1\\text{ sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Use the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny).  \nThe remaining points form the triangle \\(T_{m-1}\\) with odd size \\(m-1\\).  \nBy Lemma 1, \\(T_{m-1}\\) admits a covering with \\(m-1\\) sunny lines.  \nTogether we obtain a covering of \\(T_m\\) with exactly \\(m-1\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be given and let \\(k\\) satisfy the bound (3).\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\\qquad (0\\le t\\le n)\n\\]\n\nand take the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(t\\) lines are non‑sunny and cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).  \nThe uncovered points constitute a translate of the triangle \\(T_{n-t}=T_k\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd* (hence \\(k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd), Lemma 1 provides a covering of this remaining triangle by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even* (which can happen only when \\(k\\le n-1\\)), Lemma 2 supplies a covering of the remaining triangle by \\(k-1\\) sunny lines; together with the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines we obtain a total of \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The exceptional case \\(k=n-1\\) for odd \\(n\\)\n\nAssume \\(n\\) is odd and \\(k=n-1\\).  \nIf a covering with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines existed, at least one line would be non‑sunny.  \nRemoving that line leaves a set of points forming a triangle of even size \\(n-1\\).  \nBy Lemma 2 such a triangle cannot be covered solely by sunny lines; consequently a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is impossible when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nCombining the existence part with the impossibility proved above, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\};\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd:  } &&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
