o covering lemmas.**  \n\n   **Lemma\u202f1 (odd size).**  \n   For every odd integer \\(m\\ge3\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) *sunny* lines.\n\n   *Proof.*  Induction on the odd integer \\(m\\).\n\n   *Base \\(m=3\\).*  The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+2\n   \\]\n\n   contain respectively \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\), \\(\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\); together they cover all six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  Let \\(m\\ge5\\) be odd and assume the statement true for \\(m-2\\).  \n   Consider the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y=-2x+m+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because its slope is \\(-2\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\), \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n   The integer points of \\(T_m\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,m),\\;(2,m-2),\\;(3,m-4),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\tfrac{m+1}{2},1\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. exactly \\(\\frac{m+1}{2}\\) points, all belonging to the \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 of the triangle.  \n   Removing these points leaves the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{m-2}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le (m-2)+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   after translating by \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\).  \n   By the induction hypothesis \\(T_{m-2}\\) can be covered with \\(m-2\\) sunny lines.  \n   Adding \\(\\ell\\) gives a covering of \\(T_m\\) with \\(m\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n   **Lemma\u202f2 (even size).**  \n   For every even integer \\(m\\ge4\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m-1\\) sunny lines (i.e. all but one of the covering lines are sunny).\n\n   *Proof.*  Use the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n   The remaining points form the translate of \\(T_{m-1}\\), where \\(m-1\\) is odd.  \n   By Lemma\u202f1, \\(T_{m-1}\\) can be covered with \\(m-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Together we obtain a covering of \\(T_m\\) with exactly \\(m-1\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n4. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the bound (3).  \n   P