"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, **any** integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) (inclusive) can occur as the number of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *\\(S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\)* is the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \n   *A line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).*\n\n2. **Zero sunny lines are possible.**  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n   \\]\n\n   are distinct, none of them is sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **All\u2011sunny configurations exist.**  \n   We construct \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that together cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Step\u202f3.1 \u2013 Row\u202f1.*  \n   Row\u202f1 (the set \\(\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\)) contains \\(n\\) points. Because no line is horizontal, each line can meet this row in at most one point; therefore a covering of \\(S_n\\) by sunny lines must contain **exactly** one line through each point \\((i,1)\\).  \n\n   *Step\u202f3.2 \u2013 Choosing slopes.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) pick a rational number  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   such that the \\(n\\) slopes \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_n\\) are pairwise distinct and none of them equals \\(\\infty\\) (i.e. no vertical line).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y-1=m_i\\,(x-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through \\((i,1)\\) and, because \\(m_i\\neq0,-1\\), it is sunny. Distinct slopes guarantee that the lines are distinct.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.3 \u2013 Covering the remaining