e \\(m=2r+1\\).  Define the following \\(m\\) lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\ell_i\\colon y = x + i      &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,r+1),\\\\[2mm]\n   &\\ell'_j\\colon y = -2x + (2r+3-j) &&(j=1,2,\\dots ,r).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are sunny because their slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\), neither of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   For a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(\\ell_i\\) inside \\(T_m\\) are those with\n   \\[\n   a\\ge 1,\\qquad b=a+i,\\qquad a+b\\le 2r+2,\n   \\]\n   i.e. \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,r+1-i\\).  Hence \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly \\(r+1-i\\) points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n   For a fixed \\(j\\) the points of \\(\\ell'_j\\) inside \\(T_m\\) satisfy\n   \\[\n   a\\ge 1,\\qquad b=-2a+2r+3-j,\\qquad b\\ge 1,\n   \\]\n   which gives \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,r+1-j\\).  Thus \\(\\ell'_j\\) contains exactly \\(r+1-j\\) points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n   Counting the points contributed by all lines we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{r+1}(r+1-i)+\\sum_{j=1}^{r}(r+1-j)\n   =\\frac{(r+1)r}{2}+\\frac{r(r+1)}{2}\n   =r(r+1)=\\frac{(2r+1)(2r+2)}2=|T_m|.\n   \\]\n\n   The sets of points belonging to different lines are disjoint, because the two families have different slopes.  Consequently the \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}\\cup\\{\\ell'_j\\}\\) partition \\(T_m\\).  \u220e\n\n   *Lemma\u202f2 (even size).*  \n   For every even integer \\(m\\ge 4\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m-1\\) sunny lines together with one non\u2011sunny line (hence by \\(m\\) distinct lines of which precisely \\(m-1\\) are sunny).\n\n   *Proof.*  Write \\(m=2r\\).  Use the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\colon x=1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is non\u2011sunny.  Removing the points with \\(a=1\\) from \\(T_m\\) and translating the remaining points by \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\) yields the set \\(T_{m-1}=T_{2r-1}\\), which is odd.  By Lemma\u202f1 the latter can be covered by \\(m-1\\) sunny lines.  Adding the vertical line \\(v\\) gives a covering of \\(T_m