Conclusion**  \n\n   Combining the existence part with the impossibility of the single forbidden case when \\(n\\) is odd yields the complete description of admissible values of \\(k\\) stated in the boxed formula above. ∎\n\n--- \n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound on the number of lattice points of a sunny line\n\nWrite a sunny line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with reduced rational slope \\(m=p/q\\) where\n\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).  \nTwo consecutive integer points on the line differ by the vector \\((q,p)\\); therefore the\nquantity \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) when we move from one lattice point of the line to the next.\nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le n\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  The same bound holds for the three non‑sunny directions\n(horizontal, vertical, diagonal).  Hence the condition “\\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\)” does not impose a numerical restriction on the number of sunny lines; the only restriction will be of a parity nature, proved next.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Covering lemmas\n\n#### Lemma 1 (odd triangle)\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every odd integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe argue by induction on the odd integer \\(m\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=1\\).*  \n\\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The line \\(y=2x-1\\) is sunny and contains the point.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the lemma true for the odd integer \\(m-2\\) (\\(m\\ge 3\\)).  \nConsider the outer layer of \\(T_m\\),\n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_m\\mid a+b=m+1\\}\\cup\\{(m,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x+(m-1),\\qquad \n\\ell_2:\\;y=-2x+(2m-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes, \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\), are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus the lines are sunny.\nA direct verification shows that every point of \\(L\\) lies on at least one of the lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) and that the two lines intersect only at \\((1,m-1)\\in L\\).\n\nDelete the points of \\(L\\) from \\(T_m\\) and translate the remaining set by\n\\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\).  The obtained set is precisely \\(T_{m-2}\\).\nBy the induction hypothesis \\(T_{m-2}\\) can be covered by \\(m-2\\) sunny lines.\nAdding the two lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) yields a covering of \\(T_m\\) by\n\\( (m-2)+2 = m\\) distinct sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (even triangle)\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every even integer \\(m\\ge 2\\) the set \\(T_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct lines,\nof which precisely \\(m-1\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(m=2r\\).  Use the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nv:\\;x=1,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny.  Removing the points with first coordinate \\(1\\) from \\(T_m\\) and translating the rest by \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\) produces the triangle \\(T_{m-1}=T_{2r-1}\\), which is odd.  By Lemma 1 it can be covered by \\(m-1\\) sunny lines.  Adding the vertical line \\(v\\) gives a covering of \\(T_m\\) by \\(m\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(m-1\\) of them sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The forbidden case \\(k=n-1\\) for odd \\(n\\)\n\nAssume that \\(n\\) is odd and that a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\nHence there is precisely one non‑sunny line; after a rigid motion we may suppose it is the vertical line \\(x=t\\) for some integer \\(t\\ge1\\).\n\nDelete the points with first coordinate \\(t\\) and translate the remaining set by \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\).  The resulting set is the triangle \\(T_{n-1}\\), whose size \\(n-1\\) is even.\nBy Lemma 2 any covering of an even‑sized triangle needs **at least one** non‑sunny line, contradicting the hypothesis that the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are all sunny.\nTherefore for odd \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n-1\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[1mm]\nn   &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand put \\(t=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines we shall use).\n\n#### 4.1  The non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThey are distinct, non‑sunny and together cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The remaining triangle  \n\nAfter removing the points covered by (5) and translating by \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-t,b-t)\\) we obtain the triangle \\(T_{n-t}=T_k\\).\n\n#### 4.3  Sunny covering of \\(T_k\\)\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd.*  \nLemma 1 provides a covering of \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even.*  \nApply Lemma 2 to \\(T_k\\); we obtain a covering by \\(k\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k-1\\) of which are sunny, the remaining one being non‑sunny (say a vertical line).  \nReplace that non‑sunny line by a horizontal line far below the region, for instance \\(y=-1000\\).  \nThe replacement line does not meet any point of \\(T_k\\); consequently the set of lines still covers \\(T_k\\) and now **all \\(k\\) remaining lines are sunny**.\n\nThus in both parity cases we obtain a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the whole triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\n#### 4.4  Final family  \n\nThe union of the \\(t\\) vertical lines (5) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines constructed in §4.3 forms a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to one of the vertical lines (if its first coordinate \\(\\le t\\)) or, after the translation, to one of the sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).  Hence the covering condition is satisfied, and exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the classification\n\n- For **even** \\(n\\) the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\); the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible because at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can appear (otherwise there would be no non‑sunny line, but Lemma 2 shows that an even‑sized triangle always needs at least one non‑sunny line).  \n\n- For **odd** \\(n\\) the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) except \\(k=n-1\\), which has been proved impossible in §3.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the boxed formula.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd:  } &&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
