ce both \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are non‑zero and\n\\(p+q\\neq0\\), moving one step in direction \\((p,q)\\) changes the sum\n\\(x+y\\) by \\(p+q\\neq0\\).  Consequently the segment would intersect the side\n\\(x+y=n+1\\) at a point strictly between the two lattice points, contradicting\nthe fact that the side is met only at the endpoints of the segment.  Hence at\nmost two lattice points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequence of Lemma 1  \n\nIf a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines is used, the total number of points of\n\\(S_n\\) that they can cover is at most \\(2k\\).  Since every point of \\(S_n\\) must\nbe covered, we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n2k\\;\\ge\\;|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) we have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\); because \\(k\\le n\\) (there are only\n\\(n\\) lines in total), inequality (1) forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 3\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge4 .\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) we have \\(|S_3|=6\\) and (1) gives \\(k\\ge3\\); together with the\nobvious bound \\(k\\le3\\) we obtain \\(k=3\\) as the only possible value among\n\\(k\\ge3\\).  Thus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the only candidates are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence constructions\n\nWe now exhibit, for an arbitrary \\(n\\ge3\\), families of \\(n\\) distinct lines\nrealising each of the four values.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  \\(k=0\\) (no sunny line)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny, and a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll points with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-1\\) are covered.  \nThe only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  \nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ny = x-n+1 \\qquad(\\text{slope }1),\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((n,1)\\).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  \\(k=2\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered points are \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \nChoose two distinct sunny lines through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y =  x-n+1 \\quad(\\text{slope }1),\\\\\nL_2 &: y = 2x-2n+2 \\quad(\\text{slope }2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBoth lines contain \\((n,1)\\) and are not parallel to any forbidden direction.\nThus exactly two lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  \\(k=3\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n\\]\n\nThe remaining points form the set \\(S_3\\):\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe following three sunny lines cover \\(S_3\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y =  x+2      &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x+5    &&\\text{slope }-2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,3) & L_1\\\\\n(2,2) & L_1\\\\\n(3,1) & L_1\\\\\n(1,2) & L_2\\\\\n(3,1) & L_2\\\\\n(2,1) & L_3\\\\\n(1,1) & L_3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nso every element of \\(S_3\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and none\nof the three slopes equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical\nlines yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly three sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exclusion of all other values\n\nAssume that a configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists for some\n\\(n\\ge3\\).  By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most two points of\n\\(S_n\\); therefore the total number of points covered by the sunny lines is at\nmost \\(2k\\).  The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, and each of them can\ncontain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (a vertical line, for in