  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point of }T & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & L_1\\\\\n   (2,2) & L_1\\\\\n   (1,3) & L_2\\\\\n   (2,1) & L_2\\\\\n   (3,1) & L_3\\\\\n   (1,2) & L_3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   None of the three lines has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Adding the\n   \\(n-3\\) vertical lines yields a configuration with exactly three sunny lines.\n\n5. **Excluding all other values.**  \n   Lemma 1 together with inequality (1) shows that for any \\(n\\ge 3\\) a\n   configuration can contain at most three sunny lines; for \\(n=3\\) it must\n   contain at least three.  Hence no integer \\(k\\notin\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) can be\n   realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n*If \\(L\\) is a sunny line, then \\(L\\) meets each of the three families*\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=n+1\\}\n\\]\n\n*in at most one point of \\(S_n\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nAssume that \\(L\\) meets two distinct vertical lines \\(x=c_1\\) and \\(x=c_2\\)\n(\\(c_1\\neq c_2\\)) in points of \\(S_n\\).  Those two points have the same\n\\(y\\)-coordinate, because a line intersecting two different vertical lines\nmust be horizontal; consequently the slope of \\(L\\) would be \\(0\\), contradicting\nthe definition of a sunny line.  The same argument applied to the horizontal\nfamily shows that a sunny line cannot meet two distinct horizontal lines of\n\\(S_n\\).  Finally, if \\(L\\) met two distinct lines of the diagonal family\n\\(x+y=n+1\\), the segment joining the two intersection points would be parallel\nto \\(x+y=0\\), i.e. would have slope \\(-1\\), again impossible.  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a configuration consist of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nEvery non‑sunny line belongs to one of the three families \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal\nH,\\mathcal D\\); such a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the\nlargest possible column, row or diagonal).  By Lemma 1 each sunny line can\ncontain at most three points of \\(S_n\\) – at most one from each family.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{3k}_{\\text{points on sunny lines}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{(n-k)\\,n}_{\\text{points on non‑sunny lines}}\n\\;\\ge\\;\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 4\\) the right–hand side of (1) exceeds \\(3n\\); solving (1) for \\(k\\)\ngives \\(k\\le 3\\).  When \\(n=3\\) we have \\(|S_3|=6\\); (1) forces\n\\(k\\ge 3\\) while the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) yields \\(k\\le 3\\).  Consequently,\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions attaining the four values\n\nWe now exhibit, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n  \\]\n\n  are all non‑sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n  hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only point of \\(S_n\\) not covered is \\((n,1)\\).  Add the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=x-n+1\\qquad(\\text{slope }1),\n  \\]\n\n  which passes through \\((n,1)\\).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\).  \n  The uncovered points are  \n\n  \\[\n  (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1).\n  \\]\n\n  No two of them lie on a non‑sunny line, so we cover them with two distinct\n  sunny lines, for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=-2x+2n+1\\quad\\text{(through }(n,1),(n-1,3)),\n  \\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=\\tfrac12x-\\tfrac{n}{2}\\quad\\text{(through }(n-1,1),(n,\\tfrac12)).\n  \\]\n\n  Both slopes (\\(-2\\) and \\(\\tfrac12\\)) differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); together\n  with the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines we obtain exactly two sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n  The remaining points form the set  \n\n  \\[\n  T=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}=S_3 .\n  \\]\n\n  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n  \\]\n\n  cover \\(T\\) as shown below:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c}\n  \\text{point of }T & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,1) & L_1\\\\\n  (2,2) & L_1\\\\\n  (1,3) & L_2\\\\\n  (2,1) & L_2\\\\\n  (3,1) & L_3\\\\\n  (1,2) & L_3\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  None of the three lines has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Adding the\n  \\(n-3\\) vertical lines yields a configuration with exactly three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exclusion of all other values\n\nLemma 1 implies inequality (1), which forces \\(k\\le 3\\) for every \\(n\\ge 4\\)\nand \\(k=3\\) for \\(n=3\\).  Hence no integer \\(k\\notin\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) can be\nrealised.  The explicit constructions above show that each of the four values\n\\(0,1,2,3\\) does occur.  Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny\nlines is precisely \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\). ∎"
